<problem>
<variantControl numVariants="1" />
<!-- <title>Cobweb linear approx: nonlinear dynamical system</title> -->

<setup>

<math name="f_x">f(x)</math>
<math name="L_c" simplify="full">f(c) + m(x-c)</math>
<math name="L_c2" simplify="full">f(c) + f'(c)(x-c)</math>
<math name="L_E" simplify="full">f(E)+a(x-E)</math>
<math name="L_Ealt" simplify="full">E+a(x-E)</math>
<math name="L_E2" simplify="full">f(E)+f'(E)(x-E)</math>
<math name="L_E2alt" simplify="full">E+f'(E)(x-E)</math>
<math name="E">E</math>
<math name="one">1</math>
<math name="n1">-1</math>
<number fixed="true" name="xmin">-10</number>
<number fixed="true" name="xmax">10</number>

</setup>

  
<p>The general form of a discrete dynamical system is a dynamical system in terms of a function <m>f</m>:
  <me>x_{n+1} = f(x_n), \quad \text{for $ n=0,1,2,\ldots $ }.</me>
</p>

  
<p>To answer the questions, you can use this optional cobweb applet, where you can change the function <m>f</m></p>

  
<p>f(x) = <mathInput prefill="0.05(x+10)^2-7" name="formula" /></p>

<p>You also move the <text extend="$A.styleDescriptionWithNoun" /> to move the tangent line to an equilibrium.  Notice how the cobwebbing near an equilibrium is close to cobwebbing based on the tangent line.</p>

<p>Cobweb applet for
  <me>x_{n+1} = $$f(x_n)</me>
</p>


  <setup>
    <function name="f" symbolic="true">$formula</function>
    <solveEquations variables="x" name="solveForE" minVar="$xmin" maxVar="$xmax">$formula = x</solveEquations>
    <math extend="$A.x" name="px" />
    <derivative name="df">$f</derivative>
    <number name="numPoints">10</number>
    <number name="zeroFixed" fixed="true">0</number>
    <point x="0" y="$initialPoint.x" name="initialPoint" hide="true">
      
        <constrainToGraph />
      
    </point>


  </setup>

  <updateValue name="addLine" target="$numPoints" newValue="$numPoints+1" type="number">
    <label>Add line</label>
  </updateValue>
  <updateValue name="deleteLine" target="$numPoints" newValue="max(1,$numPoints-1)" type="number" disabled="$numPoints &lt;= 1">
    <label>Delete line</label>
  </updateValue>

  <graph identicalAxisScales="true" grid="true" showNavigation="false" xmin="$xmin" xmax="$xmax" ymin="$xmin" ymax="$xmax">
    <xLabel><m>x_n</m></xLabel>
    <yLabel><m>x_{n+1}</m></yLabel>
  <point name="A" x="0" y="0" styleNumber="3">
    
      <repeat for="$solveForE.solutions" valueName="v"><attractTo><point>($v, $v)</point></attractTo></repeat>
      <constrainTo>$f</constrainTo>
      <constrainToGraph />
    
  </point>
      <line equation="y=$$df($px)(x-$px) + $$f($px)" fixed="true" styleNumber="3" name="tangentLine" />

    <function extend="$f" layer="1" styleNumber="2" />
    <cobwebPolyline function="$f" numPoints="$numPoints" name="cobwebPolyline" initialPoint="$initialPoint" lockToSolution="true" draggable="false" />
    <point extend="$initialPoint" layer="2" hide="false" />
  </graph>

  <aside name="calculatedValue">
    <title>Calculated values</title>

    <md>
    <repeat for="$cobwebPolyline.iterateValues" valueName="x" indexName="i">
        <mrow>
          x_{<number>$i-1</number>} \amp = <math displayDigits="5" extend="$x" />
        </mrow>
      </repeat>
    </md>

  </aside>

  


<ol>
<li>
Is it possible for a nonlinear system <m>x_{n+1}=f(x_n)</m> to have zero equilibria? 
<answer inline="true">
<choiceInput>
<choice credit="1">yes</choice>
<choice>no</choice>
</choiceInput>
</answer>
  One equilibrium? 
<answer inline="true">
<choiceInput>
<choice credit="1">yes</choice>
<choice>no</choice>
</choiceInput>
</answer>
  Two or more equilibria?  
<answer inline="true">
<choiceInput>
<choice credit="1">yes</choice>
<choice>no</choice>
</choiceInput>
</answer>
 Graphically, how can you tell how many equilibria there are?  You look for the number of 
<answer inline="true">
<choiceInput shuffleOrder="true">
<choice credit="1">intersections</choice>
<choice>sunsets</choice>
<choice>inches</choice>
<choice>points</choice>
<choice>iterations</choice>
<choice>derivatives</choice>
<choice>functions</choice>
<choice>graphs</choice>
<choice>axes</choice>
<choice>units</choice>
<choice>equations</choice>
<choice>hours</choice>
<choice>times</choice>
<choice>half lives</choice>
<choice>doubling times</choice>
<choice>curves</choice>
<choice>slopes</choice>
<choice>orientations</choice>
</choiceInput>
</answer>
 between the graph of 
<answer>
<award symbolicEquality="true">f</award>
<award symbolicEquality="true">$f_x </award>
</answer>
 and the graph of the 
<answer inline="true">
<choiceInput shuffleOrder="true">
<choice credit="1">diagonal</choice>
<choice>function itself</choice>
<choice>inverse</choice>
<choice>derivative</choice>
<choice>reciprocal</choice>
<choice>trajectory</choice>
<choice>solution</choice>
<choice>equilibria</choice>
<choice>answer</choice>
<choice>universe</choice>
</choiceInput>
</answer>
.
</li>

  
<li>
<p>
Assume that the derivative of some function <m>f(x)</m> at <m>x=c</m> is <m>f'(c)=m</m>.  Given that information, write down the formula for the linear approximation (or tangent line) of <m>f(x)</m> calculated at <m>x=c</m>.
</p>
  
<p>
<m>L_c(x)=</m> 
<answer>
<award>$L_c </award>
<award credit="0.5" name="award33">$L_c2 </award>
</answer></p>
<feedback condition="$award33">Yes, this is correct.  But replace <m>f'(c)</m> with <m>m</m> for full credit.</feedback>
</li>

<li>
<p>
If we want to analyze the behavior of the dynamical system near an equilibrium <m>x=E</m>, we should look at the tangent line to <m>f</m> evaluated at <m>x=</m> 
<answer>
<award symbolicEquality="true">$E </award>
</answer>
.  If we assume that <m>f'(E)=a</m>, write down the formula for this linear approximation.
</p>

<p>
<m>L_E(x)=</m> 
<answer>
<award>$L_E </award>
<award symbolicEquality="true">$L_Ealt </award>
<award credit="0.5" name="award36">$L_E2 </award>
<award symbolicEquality="true" credit="0.5" name="award37">$L_E2alt </award>
</answer></p>
<feedback condition="$award36">Yes, this is correct.  But replace <m>f'(E)</m> with <m>a</m> for full credit.</feedback>
<feedback condition="$award37">Yes, this is correct.  But replace <m>f'(E)</m> with <m>a</m> for full credit.</feedback>
</li>

<li>
<p>
Since we can approximate <m>f</m> by its tangent line near the equilibrium, what conditions on the slope <m>f'(E)=a</m> of the tangent line will give you a stable equilibrium?
</p>
<p>

The equilibrium <m>E</m> will be stable if 
<answer>
<award symbolicEquality="true">$n1 </award>
</answer>
 <m>\lt f'(E) \lt</m> 
<answer>
<award symbolicEquality="true">$one </award>
</answer>
.
</p>

<p>What conditions on the slope of the tangent line will give you an unstable equilibrium?
</p>
<p>

The equilibrium <m>E</m> will be unstable if <m>f'(E) \gt</m> 
<answer>
<award symbolicEquality="true">$one </award>
</answer>
 or <m>f'(E) \lt</m> 
<answer>
<award symbolicEquality="true">$n1 </award>
</answer>
.</p>

<p>To reiterate, the critical quantity is the slope of the tangent line or the 
<answer inline="true">
<choiceInput>
<choice credit="1">derivative</choice>
<choice>value</choice>
</choiceInput>
</answer>
 of <m>f</m> evaluated at <m>x=</m> 
<answer>
<award symbolicEquality="true">$E </award>
</answer>
.</p>
</li>
</ol>


<solution>
<ol>
<li>yes, yes, yes, intersections, <m>f(x)</m>, diagonal
</li>
  
<li>
<m>L_c(x)=f(c)+f'(x)(x-c)=$L_c </m>.
</li>

<li>
<m>L_E(x)=f(E)+f'(E)(x-E) = $L_E </m>.
</li>

<li>
The equilibrium <m>E</m> will be stable if <m>-1 \lt f'(E) \lt 1</m>.

<p>The equilibrium <m>E</m> will be unstable if <m>f'(E) \gt 1</m> or <m>f'(E) \lt -1</m>.</p>

<p>The critical quantity is the slope of the tangent line or the derivative of <m>f</m> evaluated at <m>x=E</m>.</p>
</li>
</ol>
</solution>


</problem>